### ETH-20: sepolia_balance.py sends a descriptive User-Agent

- Public Sepolia RPC endpoints (publicnode, drpc, 1rpc, rpc.sepolia.org,
  rpc2.sepolia.org, blastapi) answer HTTP 403 to urllib's default
  "Python-urllib/3.x" agent, so the script's compiled-in default endpoint
  failed on every node. The request now sends "aitbc-sepolia-balance/1.0",
  which publicnode accepts (verified 200 vs 403); a unit test pins the
  non-default agent against a loopback stub.

### MSG-8g — DM read falls back across stored key eras; MCP plaintext wording corrected

- `e6bdab745` fix(messaging): `aitbc.dm_client` gains `DmDecryptError` (a `ValueError` subclass raised only for the SealedBox `CryptoError` case, same message as before) and `open_dm_with_eras()`, which tries the stored secret list newest to oldest and returns the first body that opens. Only a decrypt failure advances to the next era; a malformed ciphertext, non-UTF-8 payload or `aitbc-dm-v1` header mismatch raises on the spot — a tampered or misrouted message is never retried against older keys. When no era opens, one `ValueError` names only the tried count — no key, era or ciphertext material. `dm read` (CLI, via `_dm_key_eras`) and `MessagingClient.dm_read` (SDK) both walk the wallet's full `dm-keys` era list through the shared helper, so a DM sealed to a superseded `dm_key` still opens after `dm keygen --rotate` (MSG-VERIFY-1 G2). An explicit SDK `secret_key=` keeps its single-key meaning; corrupt stores still raise `DmStoreCorruptError` untouched; the empty-store and single-era paths are unchanged.
- Same commit, docs: the "MCP layer never sees plaintext" claim was wrong — the generated `aitbc_messaging_dm_*` tools shell out to `aitbc` on the resolved host (local subprocess or `ssh` via `_run_remote`) and return its stdout in the tool result, so DM plaintext transits the MCP server process and reaches a remote MCP client; key material is still read only on the host the CLI runs on. `docs/design/msg8-direct-messages.md` §5 and `docs/features/agent-messaging-paths.md` now say so (G4), and the design's sender-copy section records that Phase 1 implements no sender copy at all (recommendation (a) deferred — G3).
- Same commit, tests: `TestOpenDmWithEras` kernel coverage in `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; CLI-era coverage (`TestDmReadEras`) in `cli/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; SDK-era coverage in `packages/py/aitbc-sdk/tests/test_messaging_dm.py` — rotation round trip on both read paths, tried-count reporting without key/ciphertext material, header-failure no-fall-through (call-count spy), tampered-ciphertext-across-all-eras, single/empty/corrupt store parity and the explicit-`secret_key` single-attempt path.

### ETH-20b — `aitbc wallet eth-balance`: typed read-only Sepolia balance/nonce command + generated MCP tool

- New command `aitbc wallet eth-balance` prints the sealed-state (`latest` block tag) `eth_getBalance` and `eth_getTransactionCount` for one address: `--address` (EIP-55 validated, mixed-case checksum failures refuse; zero address and self-reads are fine — those gates were send-safety only) or `--wallet <name|path>` (plaintext `address` field only — no decryption, no password resolution, no key access). Exactly one selector is required. The endpoint resolves `--eth-rpc-url` > `ETH_RPC_URL` env > CLI config and the endpoint's own `eth_chainId` must answer 11155111. Output: `{address, balance_wei, balance_eth, nonce, rpc}` with the masked display URL (provider keys in the path never reach error text).
- Shared helpers: `_send_wallet`/`_wallet_address_only`/`_check_rpc_chain` from `eth_send.py` and `_resolve_wallet_path` from `bridge.py` moved into `commands/wallet/eth_common.py` (public names) — no private cross-module imports; `eth-send` and `bridge` behaviour unchanged.
- User-Agent: verified live from node2 that the compiled-in publicnode endpoint accepts httpx's default `python-httpx` agent (HTTP 200, valid `eth_chainId`) — no explicit UA needed in `rpc_call` (unlike the urllib path ETH-20 fixed).
- MCP: `aitbc_wallet_eth_balance` generated into `aitbc_mcp_cli_tools_generated_wallet.py`, classified read-only (`read_only_hint=True`, `_aitbc_cli_read_tool`, no dry_run/confirm) via a new `FORCE_READ_ONLY_PATHS` entry in the generator.
- Tests: new `cli/tests/test_wallet_eth_balance.py` (15 cases: happy paths, address/checksum/selector refusals, unresolved RPC, RPC error, wallet-file read never touching `private_key`, non-Sepolia refusal, masked-URL error text); `test_wallet_eth_send.py` fixture extended for the helper move. `cli/README.md` and `cli/CLI_USAGE_GUIDE.md` list the new subcommand.


### CLI: `market list`/`providers` fall back to the local market service when the hub name is unreachable

- `ffa031662` fix(cli): on the hub itself `https://<hub>` resolves to the TLS edge, which the node cannot hairpin back to, so every hub-facing read failed with "connection refused". New `_hub_base_url` keeps an explicit scheme in `HUB_DISCOVERY_URL`, maps localhost to http, and only upgrades a bare host to https (TLS termination lives on the fronting proxy); `_candidate_bases` retries the same reads against `market_service_url` (`http://127.0.0.1:8102`). `aitbc market list` and `aitbc market providers` now work on the hub and degrade gracefully on any node whose hub edge is down. `cancel`, `offer`, `offer-list` and `offer-disable` share `_hub_base_url`, so an explicit `http(s)://` value in `HUB_DISCOVERY_URL` is honoured everywhere.
- Same commit: the `/rpc/transactions` reads in `market list`'s chain fallback switched to `get_json()` — the endpoint returns a bare JSON array that `get()` rejects, which previously turned the local-RPC fallback into a TypeError abort ("returned a JSON array, not an object").
- Tests: `tests/cli/test_market_offers.py` gains `TestHubBaseUrl` (scheme matrix) and `TestMarketListFallback` (hub refused → local market service serves the list; bare-array RPC response accepted).

### Config/CLI: hub-ward URLs resolve locally on the hub; explicit schemes honored

`aitbc.config.hub` detects when the node *is* the hub (`BLOCKCHAIN_MODE=hub`,
or `NODE_ID`/`AITBC_HOSTNAME`/`P2P_NODE_ID` matching the discovery host) and
resolves the hub origin to `HUB_LOCAL_URL` or local nginx `http://127.0.0.1`
— the public name cannot hairpin back there. `hub_origin()`/`hub_service_url()`
now honor explicit `http://` schemes instead of always upgrading to https;
new `normalize_hub_base()`/`is_hub_self()` helpers back every naive call site
(market offers/jobs/escrow/ratings/status/match, network, ipfs, dashboard,
transactions, gpu-onchain, island join). `CLIConfig.hub_blockchain_rpc_url`
derives via `hub_service_url("rpc")` and covers `HUB_P2P_HOST`-only nodes.
Fixes hub-local `market list`/`providers`, `escrow`, `status`, `match` and the
fleet-wide-dead `gpu-onchain`/`island join` hub fallbacks; followers unchanged.

### CLI: `exchange-island`/`market`/`wallet` read the array-shaped `/rpc/transactions` via `get_json`

The endpoint returns a bare JSON array; `AITBCHTTPClient.get()` rejects
non-object bodies, so `exchange-island orderbook`/`orders`/`rates`,
`market` offer resolution's chain fallback, and `wallet transactions`
all failed fleet-wide before reaching their (already shape-tolerant)
normalizers. Ops fix on hub: `island_credentials.json` `rpc_endpoint`
now `http://127.0.0.1:8202/rpc` — the previous `https://hub.aitbc.bubuit.net`
value hairpin-failed from inside the hub (fulfils the ops item CLI-3
deferred).

### CLI: aitbc pool-hub on followers used http://{hub}/pool-hub — plain HTTP against a TLS-only edge. Resolved via normalize_hub_base() → https on followers, http://127.0.0.1 on hub. The nginx /pool-hub/ mount already existed; only the scheme was wrong. Also: test_find_service_by_type_and_name now sets API_KEY_STORAGE_PATH → tmp so importing the services router no longer requires /var/lib/aitbc.

### Tests: ai submit tests no longer need a live chain — mock_config fixtures set CHAIN_ID (honored before any probe by design) and stub the paid-submit get_buyer_nonce pre-flight. Fixes 4 tests red on dev hosts since cf6d8d5fc/655a1b329.

### Ops: bridge_wallet_rotation_report analyze() now normalizes withdrawal_by_hash/sell_release_by_hash keys through _norm() — direct callers passing 0x-prefixed/mixed-case eth_tx_hash no longer get unattributed outbound legs (CLI path was already consistent via _load_ledger). ENG-2.

### Auth: aitbc.auth.api_key no longer touches the filesystem at import. api_key_manager is now a lazy module-__getattr__ singleton (get_api_key_manager(), double-checked under a lock) — `import aitbc.auth`/`import aitbc.auth.api_key` create no <API_KEY_STORAGE_PATH>.lock and no longer raise PermissionError on hosts without /var/lib/aitbc. The name still yields a real APIKeyManager; API_KEY_STORAGE_PATH set after import but before first access is now honored. ENG-1.

### Tests: auth-middleware tests could not reach the "no miner keys" state — _configured_miner_keys() also merges coordinator_api.config.settings.miner_api_keys, and the Settings singleton bakes MINER_API_KEYS at first import (inside an earlier monkeypatched test). _no_miner_keys() clears both sources; the previously-red 401-log test now passes on hosts where coordinator_api is importable (node2, IDE venv). Also adds a merge/dedup contract test for _configured_miner_keys. ENG-3.

### Ops: bridge-leaf-audit reads `target_recipient` from sealed envelopes (AUDIT-RECIP-1). Sealed lock envelopes carry the recipient as `target_recipient` (`bridge_transfer.py`); the audit's `derived_leaf` only read `recipient`, so every lock sealed at/above `bridge_lock_leaf_v2_height` derived `unreadable` and classified `missing_data` — a false alarm, not a chain defect (LEAF-V2-LOCK-1 proved the v2 leaf correct end-to-end). `derived_leaf` now accepts the alias in payload and envelope; the skipped-trie line explains the `PYTHONPATH` requirement.

### CLI: `bridge proof`/`sign-proof`/`ingest-header`/`attest` no longer crash on the success line (CLI-PROOF-OUT-1). Each bound `--output` to a parameter named `output`, shadowing the `output()` formatter — the command wrote its file then raised `'str' object is not callable` (on `sign-proof` without `-o`, `None(...)` — unconditional). The options now bind to `output_path`; flag names unchanged. CliRunner tests pin the success path for `proof` and `sign-proof`.

### CLI: `bridge unlock --wallet-name` signs the refund in-process, like `lock` (CLI-UNLOCK-SIGN-1). `unlock` previously required a raw `--signature` built outside the CLI; it now accepts `--wallet-name`/`--wallet-password` and signs `{transfer_id, sender, action:"unlock"}` with the wallet key — the exact dict the server verifies. `--signature` stays accepted; both-given and a sender/wallet-address mismatch are refused before any request. Signatures and keys are never printed.

### Escrow: `fund_contract` no longer marks a contract FUNDED on a hash it never read (ESC-FUND-1). `escrow/create` passed the mempool-acceptance hash straight through; a lock evicted before sealing left a FUNDED contract whose settlement gates then 425'd forever on a hash that never lands. `escrow_routes._verify_funding_lock` now re-loads the lock — sealed `transactions` row first, hub `/mempool` otherwise — and requires the contract's job_id, buyer, amount and escrow destination; absence is retryable (425), a field mismatch or a different sealed hash is a conflict (409), and on refusal the just-created contract is dropped so the retry starts clean. `fund_contract` cross-checks the verified tx's hash and stores it on the contract.

### Escrow: sealed-lock funding verify reads the sealed row's real keys (ESC-FUND-1 fix). `b0fed2982`'s `_assert_lock_matches_contract` read `from`/`to`/`amount` — the submitted-body shape — but a sealed `transactions` row carries `sender`/`recipient`/`value`, so a lock sealing between submit and verify got a false 409 and a dropped contract. `_canonical_lock_fields` now normalizes both shapes before the field check; the mempool branch is unchanged. The `_sealed()` test fixture is rebuilt in the real row shape (keys from a live read).

### Bridge: the lock signature now carries `issued_at` + a single-use `nonce` (SEC-REPLAY-1). The six-field lock dict `{source_chain, target_chain, sender, recipient, amount, asset}` had no freshness or nonce, so a captured signed body replayed in a later second minted a new `transfer_id` (it derives from `time.time()`) — debiting the sender and fee again. Wallet-signed locks (`bridge lock --wallet-name`) now sign and send `issued_at` (within ±`_ADMIN_MAX_SKEW_SECS`) and a random 16-byte hex nonce, keyed `bridge-lock:<sender>:<nonce>` in the shared sqlite nonce store — replay of the same body is refused once spent, and any body carrying the new fields is always freshness+nonce-checked regardless of flags. Old-form locks stay accepted while `BRIDGE_LOCK_REQUIRE_REPLAY_GUARD` is unset (deployed-CLI compatibility); setting it refuses old-form locks (400). Raw `--signature` submissions stay old-form for the compat window — flip the flag only after callers move to `--wallet-name`. Six other handlers share the generic signing scheme; their audit table is the SEC-REPLAY follow-up list.

### Bridge: `POST /bridge/batch/lock` removed — it moved funds with no sender signature at all (BRIDGE-BATCHLOCK-AUTH-1). The route fed `{"transfers":[…]}` straight into `initiate_transfer` per item, debiting any named `sender` plus the unrefunded fee; the only barriers were nginx's non-GET allowlist on `/rpc/bridge/` and the amount cap. Nothing legitimate called it — no CLI command, MCP tool, or script used `BridgeClient.batch_lock` — so the route, `BridgeBatchRequest`, the client method, and `bridge_transfer.batch_lock` are deleted rather than retrofitted. `POST /bridge/batch/confirm` stays: it verifies a confirmer signature per item and moves no funds itself. A regression test asserts `POST /bridge/batch/lock` now 404/405s so the route cannot quietly return; if a real caller ever appears it comes back with per-item signatures from the start.

### Bridge: cross-chain lock/swap signatures gain the replay guard (SEC-REPLAY-2). `_lock_transfer` (`POST /swap`, `POST /cross-chain/bridge`) verified a fixed-field signature with no nonce or expiry — a captured body replayed as a new `transfer_id`, debiting the sender again, same flaw as the single lock. The SEC-REPLAY-1 helpers are lifted into `rpc/utils.py` (`apply_replay_guard_fields`, `check_signed_request_freshness`, `verify_signed_request_with_freshness`) and shared: guarded bodies sign `issued_at` (±300 s) + `nonce` keyed `crosschain-lock:<sender>:<nonce>` in the shared store and are always checked; old-form stays accepted until `CROSS_CHAIN_REQUIRE_REPLAY_GUARD` is set (default off). `aitbc crosschain swap`/`bridge` now sign the fields. `_ADMIN_NONCE_TTL_SECS` goes 600→660 so a nonce spent at the earliest acceptable instant still outlives the body's freshness window. Route tests run through TestClient (the real JSON path), not handler-with-dict. `agent_economics_auth` reviewed and left alone: `create_agent_stake`/`deploy_bounty` are idempotent on their ids, the rest are state-bound transitions or append-only memos — replay moves no funds.

### Security: refresh `.secrets.baseline` (SECRETS-BASELINE-1). Regenerated via `detect-secrets scan --baseline` (1.5.0, unchanged plugin/filter set): drops 12 entries for files deleted from the tree plus 5 existing files whose recorded secret no longer appears; refreshes 82 line numbers; records 110 new findings accumulated since the 23 Sep generation (64 test fixtures, 31 generated MCP tool files, 5 lockfile, 10 in source — reviewed: alembic revision hashes, env-var-name constants, config label maps; none are live credentials). No operator verdicts existed to preserve; none flipped.

### Bridge: follower-side `bridge_state_root` shadow check (BRIDGE-ROOT-FOLLOW-1). Imported blocks carrying a root were stored verbatim — a follower never re-derived what the sender attached, and equal block hashes prove header agreement, not root correctness. New flag-gated `cross_chain/bridge_root_shadow.run_shadow_check` (off by default, `BRIDGE_ROOT_SHADOW_CHECK`): at/above the V-4 boundary it re-derives the trie root from the sealed BRIDGE_LOCK txs only — payload first, `envelope` fallback for GAP-47 rows, never the local `CrossChainTransfer` table (a lagging table would manufacture false mismatches) — and compares; a mismatch logs `[BRIDGE-ROOT-SHADOW]` with height + 12-hex root prefixes and counts `bridge_root_shadow_mismatch_total` (`_checked_total`, `_underivable_total`, `_errors_total` alongside). Compare-only: never rejects, never stores a different root, blocks below 30000 skipped, flag-off returns before touching a tx. Enforcement is a later, separate decision.

