### EXPL-STATS-1 — explorer-api network-stats: 503 on failed read + anchored_offers rename

`GET /api/analytics/network-stats` no longer answers 200 with a
zero-filled body when the chain database is missing or unreadable — both
failure paths raise HTTP 503 ("chain database unavailable" / "network
stats unavailable") so a failed read can never look like an empty chain.
The success body adds `anchored_offers` (distinct offer keys ever
anchored across GPU_MARKET/GPU_MARKETPLACE/GPU_REGISTER) and keeps
`active_offers` as a deprecated alias with the same value; removal waits
on the website reading the new name (WEB-ANCHORED-1) and docs. Tests
cover the dual-name shape and all three failure cases.

### EXPL-STATS-2 — network-stats: _chain_db_path() OSError answers 503, not 500

An `OSError` raised by `_chain_db_path()` itself (e.g. `PermissionError`
on the data directory) previously escaped `api_network_stats` before the
`try`, surfacing as an anonymous 500 while every other failure of the
route answers 503. The path lookup now runs inside the `try`, so the
same failure answers 503 "network stats unavailable" with no path or
exception text in the body.

### OLDFORM-COUNTER-1 — count old-form signed bodies on replay-guarded routes

`blockchain_replay_guard_requests_total{endpoint, form}` counts signed
bodies on `/bridge/lock` and the cross-chain lock routes by form
(`new_form`, `legacy_accepted`, `legacy_refused`), so the
`BRIDGE_LOCK_REQUIRE_REPLAY_GUARD` flip can rest on observed traffic
rather than a code census. `legacy_accepted` increments only after the
signature verifies — unauthenticated traffic cannot fake live old-form
signers — and `legacy_refused` counts inside the flag's 400 path. First
`legacy_accepted` per endpoint per process also logs one WARNING.
Counter is process-local, resets on rpc restart, and is not live until a
separate rollout.
